 "tier": "B",
 "accessed": "2026-11-20",
 "note": "WHO's estimates put every country on one method so they can be compared. For a country that registers nearly every death with its cause, the estimate follows those records closely. For a country that registers few, the line is mostly WHO's model. Each country is labelled by how many years of its own registered deaths WHO had to work from.",
 "grades": {
  "registered": "15 or more of the 22 years: the line rests mainly on the country's own death records.",
  "partly registered": "5 to 14 years: part counted, part modelled.",
  "estimate": "Fewer than 5 years: the line is WHO's model, not counted deaths.",
  "aggregate": "A region or the world: a sum of the countries in it, counted and modelled alike."
 },
 "caveats": [
  "2020 and 2021 include the COVID-19 pandemic; WHO counts COVID-19 deaths as their own cause.",
  "Dementia's rise is partly a change in how death certificates are written, not only more deaths.",
  "Sepsis is not one of WHO's causes: a death is counted under the illness that led to the infection. Only sepsis in newborns has its own line.",
  "These are WHO's estimates, which differ from each country's own published rates because they use the world standard population.",
  "The newest year is 2021. Countries' own records run later; see the reporting gap on the page."
 ],
